gest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags.\n+**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`.\n \n **One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n \n@@ -420,7 +420,7 @@ Agents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit\n - **Merges are gated.** The merger lands a PR only when every review passed and required checks are green on an up-to-date branch; protected paths wait for a human approval. The App bypasses `main`'s ruleset (its direct `.ard/` commits need it), so the merger's own check that required checks are green is what gates a merge, backed by the bot-commit audit.\n - **Agent text cannot close issues.** Agent text in a commit message, PR title or PR body has closing references neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`: \"Fixes #12\" becomes \"Fixes issue 12\"). Only a closing keyword directly before a reference closes an issue on GitHub, so only those are rewritten, including the HTML-entity (`&#35;12`, `&#x23;12`, `&num;12`) and backslash-escaped (`\\#12`) forms, which markdown renders as `#12`; \"fixed a typo in #12's test\" is left alone. Only ard's own `Closes #<issue>` on the worker PR closes an issue.\n - **Worker diffs are scanned.** The worker's diff is checked for known credential values and for secret patterns on the lines the job added. A hit holds the change for a human (the comment names file, line and rule, never text). Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do; a literal is held.\n-- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\n+- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\n - **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. At the cap the controller stops claiming and posts once on the status issue.\n - **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n - **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open